i) Introducion

The TMI-3 channel daemon is very unique. Instead of having
one big daemon that handles the different channel networks
(including intermud and local channel networks), the TMI-3
channel daemon has a modular design. This allows for more
control and simplicity and is very powerful.

ii) Components

chan_d.c [/adm/daemons/chan_d.c]
<different modules>

iii) Technical Details

The channel daemon has five functions.

     a) int register(string name, string abrev, string module, string *ch_list);

Modules make a call out to this function to register it's self and it's
channels. It passes it's name, abreviation, full path to module, and an
array of it's channels. It will return 0 for failure, 1 if registered.

     b) int snd_msg(string chan, string usr, string msg);

This function is called by user.c when a user types something into
the mud. It will check to see if it should be sent to a channel and
will do so. The function requres the channel name, user who sent the
message, and the message. It will return 1 if message sent and 0 if 
not a channel.

     c) void rec_msg(string chan, string msg);

This function is called by the module when it recieves a msg.
This function will send the message to the listening users
It requires the module to send the channel name and the msg
it wants the listeners to see.

     d) void tune(string chan, string name, int flag);

This function is called to tune a user in or out of
a channel. This will most likely be called by a command
at a user's request. It requires the channel name, name
of the user, and a number designating the requested
action (1 for on, 0 for off). 

iv) How to write your own modules

To write your own module, you must be aware of how to interact
with the channel daemon. To register your module, you must call
int register(string name, string abrev, string module, string *ch_list);
in the channel daemon. You must ensure that string module is the full
path to the user object. 

You must have the following functions in your module:

     a) void ch_name(string ch_old, string ch_new);

If there is a channel that has the same name as one already registered,
then the channel name will be prefixed with the abrev that you provided.
(<abrev>_<channel name>). The daemon will call 
ch_name(string ch_old, string ch_new); in your module when it does this.
string ch_old is the old channel name and string ch_new is the new channel
name.

     b) void rec_msg(string chan, string usr, string msg);

This is called by the channel daemon to notify the module that there
is an outgoing message. string chan is the name of the channel the message
is goign out on, string usr is the name of the usr sendign it, and string msg
is the msg.

When you want to send a message to the users who are listening to a specific
channel, the module will call void rec_msg(string chan, string msg); in
the channel daemon. It is important to note that string msg passed will
be exactly what the user sees so make sure to format it before calling
the daemon.

v) Plans/Goals

Current, the daemon has no security at all to protect forged message
and such. Also, there is no clean method to update the list of channels
that the channel daemon knows about. To add new channels, you might call
register() in the channel daemon again but only have the new channels in
the array.